    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nFor True Outdoor Lovers: inTech's Tiny 2027 Flyer Explore Toy Hauler Doesn't Sacrifice Garage Space\n18 photos\nPhoto: inTech RV\nEver since the cart was created, we can say that people have been hitching rides on trailers. In time, and humans being the creative bunch that they are, we would eventually drop walls onto these carts and call them home during long arduous travels.\nFast forward to today, and the beast-drawn carriage and trailer is not so much a common sight, having been replaced with purpose-driven machines that promise to be the solution we look for when traveling away from our rooted homes.\nSome travel trailers are aimed at large families that want all the creature comforts possible, while others are aimed at smaller groups that don’t mind an experience that as raw as can be. Come to think of it, these days, we even have countless RV designs that bridge the gap between these extremes.\nPhoto: inTech RV One style that countless Americans call upon is the toy hauler, trailers which can be used in the traditional sense, to sleep, dine, and share laughs with loved ones, but which also feel very comfortable carrying a couple of dirt bikes, ATVs, rigid kayaks, e-bikes, and even some cars in some cases.\nA toy hauler is the reason we’re here today, and unlike other larger ones the market may offer, the 2027 inTech Flyer Explore is actually on the opposite end of the size spectrum and aims to be as compact as possible while still granting us a space where we can go about our daily camping duties and bring along some toys.\nNow, the sort of experience that a compact toy hauler can offer is clearly different than a larger one, so don’t expect to see any drop-down beds, massive living areas, or even the largest garage, but there are some very clear reasons why this unit needs to be on your to-consider list.\nPhoto: inTech RV First and foremost, let’s begin with the garage, an area of the unit that actually occupies almost the entirety of the floorspace, something that is rarely seen in a toy hauler as the sides are usually lined with bedding options, tables and chairs, and more.\nThe way this is made possible is by the approach inTech took toward designing the Explore. Instead of placing a kitchen inside, inTech chose to move it to the exterior of the RV and hidden and protected in the belly of the camper because it’s placed on a slideout.\nThis feature slides into the interior countertop construction that hides at the very front of the camper, just in case you’re wondering where it goes. It would have been interesting if the interior countertop could be lifted in order to access the fridge or cooktop inside.\nPhoto: inTech RV That takes care of the kitchen – the unit has no bathroom – so what about the bedding? The bed we can access in the 2027 Flyer Explore is a fold out bed, but instead of extending into the interior, it actually extends toward the exterior of the unit. This means that you don’t have to park your bike or kayaks outside just to sleep.\nKeep in mind that the larger toy haulers out there don’t usually have this ability, and we often need to leave our rides outside in order to accommodate party members in the garage. A big win for inTech I’d say.\nAnother big win is the fact that inTech advertises this camper with an MSRP of $23,800 (€21,700) in it’s most standard form, and dealers are letting them go for around $25K-$30K depending on features and options added to the mix.\nPhoto: inTech RV Moving forward with more of the Explore’s magic, allow me to point out that the entire rear wall is actually a gate that drops down and functions as a ramp for you and your gear. When it’s time to pack up, you can lift it with just one hand and lock it into place. Have a look at the clip below to see it in action.\nWhile I can talk you through the features and options that we have inside a Flyer, I’ll leave that to your dealer and instead, tell you a bit about how this camper is made. Clearly, to support the added weight of some toys, it can’t be built out of your typical wood.\nInstead, inTech goes to town building the Flyer Explore with an all-aluminum frame and body panels that are also lined with sheets of aluminum on the outside, gelcoat on top and kept in place with the help of some of the most rugged extrusions and seams I’ve seen on a camper of this size and price in a while.\nDry, these babies can weigh as little as 1,580 lbs (717 kg) and can handle nearly double that, or 2,990 lbs (1,456 kg). Maybe if you upgrade the suspension and reinforce the floor, you can push that number higher. Talk things over with a local dealer or get in touch with inTech directly. According to sources, inTech customer service and support is generally viewed as “positive.”\nPhoto: inTech RV Personally, positive is the feeling I get when looking at this three-season toy hauler, especially since it taps into some neat tricks to grant us access to a space that’s very versatile.\nI mean, if it’s not raining and you’re not worried about theft in your camping area, you can easily leave the bikes outside and toss down a couple of sleeping bags where you and your significant other can rest and the kids can take the bed. Why not use the space to set up a dining table? There’s just so much we can do with this much empty floorspace.\nThese days, travel trailers come in all shapes and sizes, but what the 2027 Flyer Explore brings to the table is versatility in it’s smallest form possible.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntravel trailer Camper RV Lifestyle mobile living american affordable\nAbout the author: Cristian Curmei\nA bit of a nomad at heart (being born in Europe and raised in several places in the USA), Cristian is enamored with travel trailers, campers and bikes. He also tests and writes about urban means of transportation like scooters, mopeds and e-bikes (when he's not busy hosting our video stories and guides). Full profile\nUP NEXT 8-Lug Pontiac Catalina 2+2 Is a One-Family Power Cruiser, Original 421 Pulls Like a Tug Boat",
